Jetpack Joyride (Indy) – JavaFX Edition
=======================================

Overview
--------
Indy is a side‑scrolling runner inspired by the original **Jetpack Joyride**.  
You control a jetpack‑equipped character flying through a lab full of zappers, rockets, lasers, and power‑ups.  
The game is built in JavaFX with a focus on smooth movement, readable visuals, and gradually increasing difficulty.

Core Gameplay
-------------
- **Goal**: Survive as long as possible while dodging obstacles and collecting power‑ups to push your high score higher.
- **Controls**:  
  - `SPACE` – Hold to thrust upward, release to fall.  
  - `P` – Pause / resume the game.  
  - Mouse – Click the on‑screen buttons to pause, restart, or quit.
- **Score**: Increases automatically as you travel farther and as the game speed ramps up.
- **Game Over**: Hitting an obstacle without an active shield ends the run; your high score is saved to `jetpackjoyride.txt`.

Power‑Ups
---------
- **Vehicle Power‑Up**  
  - Transforms you into special vehicles (Motorcycle, Stomper, Dragon, GravitySuit, etc.).  
  - Each vehicle has unique physics and movement, giving short bursts of different play styles.

- **Shield Power‑Up**  
  - Grants one or more shield charges.  
  - A glowing circle appears around the character; each shield absorbs a single hit.  
  - When a shield absorbs damage, the game briefly slows and then ramps back up so you have time to react.

- **Zoom Power‑Up**  
  - Dramatically increases speed and grants temporary invincibility.  
  - A jet‑flame / triangle visual appears behind the avatar and the world scrolls faster while you zoom.

- **Slow‑Mo Power‑Up**  
  - Temporarily slows the entire game (including the parallax background) for easier dodging.  
  - A soft bubble visual surrounds the character while slow‑mo is active.

Obstacles & Difficulty
----------------------
- **Zappers** – Static or angled electric barriers that block portions of the vertical space.  
- **Rockets** – Chase in from the right with a warning indicator before impact.  
- **Lasers** – Show two warning blocks first, then fire a solid beam between them for a short duration.

The `WorldGenerator` gradually ramps up game speed and obstacle density while enforcing:
- A maximum number of simultaneous obstacles.
- Limits on how much of the vertical space is blocked at once.
- Special patterns when you are in certain vehicles (e.g., motorcycle‑friendly layouts).

To reduce cheap deaths, the game:
- Briefly slows down after shielded hits and then smoothly recovers to the previous speed.  
- Clears active obstacles on major transformations so you can adjust to the new vehicle.  
- Uses a transformation queue so multiple vehicle power‑ups can be collected and used in order.

Visuals & UI
------------
- **Parallax Background**: Stars and streaks scroll at different speeds to give a sense of forward motion even when the screen is relatively empty.
- **HUD**:  
  - Score, high score, shield count, and upcoming transformations are displayed at the top.  
  - A short helper message explains the basic controls.  
- **Controls Bar**: Styled buttons at the bottom for **Pause/Resume**, **Restart**, and **Quit**.

Running the Game
----------------
Requirements:
- JDK 17+  
- Maven (project is configured with `javafx-maven-plugin`)

From the project root (`cs15-jetpack-joyride`):
- Run the game: `mvn clean javafx:run`  
- Run tests: `mvn test`

High‑Level Architecture (for developers)
----------------------------------------
- `App` – JavaFX entry point; creates the main window and scene.  
- `PaneOrganizer` – Top‑level UI layout (game pane, HUD, control buttons, borders).  
- `Game` – Core game loop: movement, spawning, collision detection, score, power‑ups, pause logic.  
- `WorldGenerator` – Procedural obstacle generation and difficulty/speed management.  
- `Vehicle` hierarchy – Avatar and transformation vehicles with different movement mechanics.  
- `Obstacle` hierarchy – Zapper, Rocket, Laser, etc.  
- `PowerUp` hierarchy – Vehicle, Shield, Zoom, Slow‑Mo, plus their visuals and activation behavior.

If you are extending the game, `Game`, `WorldGenerator`, and the `Vehicle`/`Obstacle`/`PowerUp` families are the main places to plug in new mechanics, visuals, or level logic.
